## 4. My Understanding

### Task 6.1 — Basic Git Workflow

| Git Operation | What It Does |
|---------------|-------------|
| Check repository status | `git status` shows the current state — which files have been changed, which are staged and which are untracked. It helps me see what will be included in the next commit. |
| Stage selected changes | `git add` moves changes from the working directory to the staging area. Staging lets me choose exactly which changes to include in a commit rather than committing everything. |
| Create a commit | `git commit` takes a snapshot of everything in the staging area and saves it permanently in the repository history with a message describing what changed. |
| View commit history | `git log` lists past commits so I can see what was changed, when and by whom. |

**Staging vs committing:** Staging (`git add`) selects which changes to include; committing (`git commit`) permanently records those selected changes. I can stage some files and leave others out, giving me control over what goes into each commit.

### Task 6.2 — Branch: `update-about`

I created the branch `update-about`, added a "My Learning" section to the root README describing things I learned (IPOS model, Terminal commands, and how the OS manages processes), committed the change and pushed the branch to GitHub.

### Task 6.3 — Pull Request

I opened a Pull Request on GitHub from `update-about` into `main`, wrote a description, reviewed the diff and merged it. After merging, I ran `git checkout main` and `git pull origin main` locally to bring the merged changes onto my computer.

**What is a branch?** A branch is an independent line of development. It lets me work on changes without affecting the main codebase. If the changes work well, they can be merged back.

**What is a Pull Request?** A Pull Request is a request on GitHub to merge one branch into another. It shows the differences and lets team members review, discuss and approve changes before they become part of the main branch. This is useful for collaboration because mistakes can be caught during review.

### Task 6.4 — Git Command Glossary

| Operation | Explanation |
|-----------|-------------|
| `git init` | Creates a new Git repository in the current folder |
| `git status` | Shows which files are changed, staged or untracked |
| `git add` | Stages changes so they are ready to be committed |
| `git commit` | Saves a snapshot of staged changes with a descriptive message |
| `git log` | Displays the history of past commits |
| `git branch` | Creates a new branch or lists existing branches |
| `git checkout` / `git switch` | Switches to a different branch |
| `git push` | Uploads local commits to the remote repository on GitHub |
| `git pull` | Downloads and merges remote changes into the local branch |

